---
/**
 * K Culture Wire — **한국 방송을 거친 시리즈와 안 거친 시리즈.** (`/two-pipelines`)
 *
 * ── ⛔ 이 지면이 지키는 것 ────────────────────────────────────
 * ⛔ **줄세우지 않는다.** 두 무리를 나란히 놓고 **왜 다른지**를 같이 적는다.
 *    「어느 쪽이 낫다」를 안 쓴다 — 방송 쪽이 자리의 60.6% 를 가진다.
 * ⛔ **대리 표지임을 화면에 적는다.** 「방송사 credit 이 없다」는 「방송을 안 탔다」가 아니다.
 * ⛔ **영화는 반대로 나온다.** 그것도 같이 낸다 — 안전장치를 숨기면 기사가 커진다.
 * ⛔ 까닭을 지어내지 않는다. 넷플릭스가 자기 작품을 미는 것이 이 꼴을 만들 수 있지만
 *    우리 자료로는 **못 가린다.** 「두 발자국이 다르다」까지만 적는다.
 * ⛔ 광고 자리를 만들지 않았다. Riot Production(App 866800) 승인 전이다.
 * ⚠ 수를 손으로 적지 않는다. 전부 `wikitip-two-pipelines.json` 에서 읽는다.
 */
import WikiTip from '../../layouts/WikiTip.astro';
import KcwRelatedArticles from '../../components/KcwRelatedArticles.astro';
import data from '../../data/wikitip-two-pipelines.json';

const A = data.aired;
const N = data.notAired;
const 배수 = +(N.medianMarkets / A.medianMarkets).toFixed(1);

const TITLE = 'Korean series that aired on a Korean network, and those that did not';
const DESC =
  `Of ${data.seriesWithFirms} charting Korean series with a company credit, ${A.titles} aired on a `
  + `Korean television network and reach a median ${A.medianMarkets} markets. The ${N.titles} with no `
  + `broadcaster credit reach a median ${N.medianMarkets}. They are ${N.titleSharePc}% of the titles `
  + `and ${N.placeSharePc}% of the chart places.`;

const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Dataset',
  name: TITLE,
  description: DESC,
  creator: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'K Culture Wire' },
  isBasedOn: ['https://www.netflix.com/tudum/top10', 'https://www.wikidata.org'],
};
---

<WikiTip title={TITLE} description={DESC} jsonLd={jsonLd}
  canonical="https://www.kculturewire.com/two-pipelines">
  <h1>{TITLE}</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    A Korean series can reach a Netflix chart by two routes: it aired on a Korean television network
    and was licensed on, or it was made for streaming and never had a broadcast run. Split the
    charting series on that line and the two halves do not look like the same business.
  </p>
  <p class="q">{data.question}</p>

  <section>
    <h2>The two groups, side by side</h2>
    <p class="note lead-in"><b>How this is counted.</b> {data.unit}</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>Aired on a Korean network</th><th>No broadcaster on record</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Series</td>
            <td>{A.titles} <span class="fine">({A.titleSharePc}%)</span></td>
            <td class="tot">{N.titles} <span class="fine">({N.titleSharePc}%)</span></td></tr>
          <tr class="edge"><td class="nm">Markets reached, median</td>
            <td>{A.medianMarkets}</td><td class="tot">{N.medianMarkets}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Chart places held, median</td>
            <td>{A.medianPlaces}</td><td class="tot">{N.medianPlaces}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Weeks on a chart somewhere, median</td>
            <td>{A.medianWeeks}</td><td class="tot">{N.medianWeeks}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Reached 20 markets or more</td>
            <td>{A.reached20}</td><td class="tot">{N.reached20}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Charted in exactly one market</td>
            <td>{A.oneMarketOnly}</td><td class="tot">{N.oneMarketOnly}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Reached number one somewhere</td>
            <td>{A.reachedNo1} <span class="fine">({A.reachedNo1Pc}%)</span></td>
            <td class="tot">{N.reachedNo1} <span class="fine">({N.reachedNo1Pc}%)</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">Chart places between them</td>
            <td>{A.places.toLocaleString('en-US')} <span class="fine">({A.placeSharePc}%)</span></td>
            <td class="tot">{N.places.toLocaleString('en-US')} <span class="fine">({N.placeSharePc}%)</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The median series without a broadcaster on record reaches {배수} times as many markets as the
      median series with one, out of {data.marketCount}. {A.oneMarketOnly} of the {A.titles} network
      series charted in a single country and never travelled at all; {N.oneMarketOnly} of the
      {' '}{N.titles} did. Between them the two groups hold
      {' '}{data.seriesPlaces.toLocaleString('en-US')} chart places.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The network route is not the losing one</h2>
    <p class="warn">
      It would be easy to read the table as one pipeline beating another. It does not say that.
      The {A.titles} network series hold <b>{A.places.toLocaleString('en-US')} chart places</b> —
      {' '}{A.placeSharePc}% of everything Korean series took — against
      {' '}{N.places.toLocaleString('en-US')} for the other group. There are simply far more of them,
      each travelling less far. <b>Two different businesses, both working.</b>
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      The two groups also differ at the top of the chart: {N.reachedNo1Pc}% of the
      no-broadcaster group reached number one somewhere against {A.reachedNo1Pc}% of the network
      group. That follows from reach — a title in {N.medianMarkets} markets has more chances at a
      number one than one in {A.medianMarkets} — and is not a separate finding.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What we are actually reading, and why it might be wrong</h2>
    <p class="warn">{data.proxyWarning}</p>
    <p class="note">
      The no-broadcaster group at 60 markets or more, alphabetically: {N.biggestExamples.join(', ')}.{' '}
      <b>Alphabetical, not ranked.</b> Read the list and judge the proxy for yourself — that is why
      it is printed rather than summarised.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Films go the other way, which is why we are not generalising</h2>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Films with a company credit</th><th>Titles</th><th>Markets reached, median</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="nm">Aired on a Korean network</td><td>{data.films.aired.titles}</td><td class="tot">{data.films.aired.medianMarkets}</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="nm">No broadcaster on record</td><td>{data.films.notAired.titles}</td><td class="tot">{data.films.notAired.medianMarkets}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Among films the direction reverses, on much smaller medians. So this is a fact about Korean
      <i>series</i>, not a fact about streaming, and we are not going to write it as one. A film's
      broadcast history is also a weaker signal — films reach television after cinemas, if at all.
    </p>
  </section>

  <KcwRelatedArticles page="/two-pipelines" />

  <section class="src">
    <h2>Where these numbers come from, and what they cannot answer</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Netflix and Wikidata</b> — {data.source}</li>
      <li><b>Why the two differ is not in here.</b> {data.cannotAnswer}</li>
      <li>
        <b>Coverage.</b> {data.seriesWithFirms} of the {data.seriesTotal} Korean series that charted
        carry any company credit at all — {data.seriesCoveragePc}%. The rest are absent from this
        table rather than small in it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>A credit is not a share of the work.</b> Wikidata records that a company is attached, not
        how much of it the company held.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <a href="/titles">Every Korean title that has charted</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/catalogue-reach">How far a company's catalogue travels</a> ·{' '}
      <a href="/about">How we count</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</WikiTip>
